# Repository line-ending policy.
#
# Text files are stored as LF in the repository AND checked out as LF.
#
# Why the global rule: on a Windows checkout git would otherwise materialise
# text files as CRLF. Pipeline tooling that writes files directly to a branch
# through the API (persist_tests / tests-commit) reads those working-tree bytes
# verbatim, bypassing git's clean filter, so main ends up holding CRLF blobs
# while the spec branch holds LF ones. Git then reports whole-file changes on
# files whose text is identical, and merges are refused. `eol=lf` makes the
# working tree LF as well, so a raw writer copies LF and the divergence cannot
# be created in the first place.
#
# This takes precedence over core.autocrlf (currently `true` on this machine),
# so no per-machine git config change is required.
* text=auto eol=lf

# UTF-8 docs carrying non-ASCII (em-dashes): git's autocrlf re-encodes these on
# checkout and produces mojibake. The rule above already covers them; these are
# kept explicit because they were the original site of that bug and are worth
# naming.
docs/agentic-pipeline/playbooks/references.md text eol=lf
docs/agentic-pipeline/common-rules.md text eol=lf
AGENTS.md text eol=lf

# Windows command scripts in the test tree are governed by the LF rule above,
# deliberately: files under .opencode/tests/** are rewritten by persist_tests,
# so a CRLF working tree there would recreate the blob divergence. If a shim
# ever mis-parses under LF (cmd.exe is line-ending sensitive with goto/labels),
# give that specific fixture `text eol=crlf` — stored LF, checked out CRLF.

# Binary assets: never convert, never attempt a text diff. `text=auto` detects
# most of these by content, but these types are byte-pinned by tests or are
# shipped model/font assets, so the intent is declared rather than inferred.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.glb   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
